      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nBefore you start, we recommend reading *Learning Multiplication Table (6 and 7)* (<htcite0>), where you learned how to multiply by six and by seven.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "600c0c57-4120-478f-b5fa-66e7ecb6372d",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Learning Multiplication Table (6 and 7)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nHello, explorer! A multiplication table is like a map of answers: once we know the map, we no longer need to count everything again. Today we will learn two more maps — the table of eight (8) and the table of nine (9).\n\nBefore we count, let's look at some shapes. A shape with straight sides is called a **polygon** (<htcite0>). We give each polygon a name by counting its sides:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b7f0c5a2-3e64-4c1d-9a8b-2f6d5e7c9a10",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Polygon",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- A **triangle** has 3 sides.\n- A **quadrilateral** has 4 sides.\n- A **pentagon** has 5 sides.\n- A **hexagon** has 6 sides.\n- A **heptagon** has 7 sides.\n- An **octagon** has 8 sides.\n- An **enneagon** (also called a **nonagon**) has 9 sides.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Look at **Figure 1**. Count the sides of each shape with your finger. The number under each shape tells you how many sides it has.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 330 440\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Polygons with 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 sides\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polygon points=\"60.0,36.0 89.4,87.0 30.6,87.0\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,36.0 199.0,70.0 165.0,104.0 131.0,70.0\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\"/><polygon points=\"270.0,36.0 302.3,59.5 290.0,97.5 250.0,97.5 237.7,59.5\" fill=\"#fff3e0\"/><polygon points=\"60.0,176.0 89.4,193.0 89.4,227.0 60.0,244.0 30.6,227.0 30.6,193.0\" fill=\"#f3e5f5\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,176.0 191.6,188.8 198.1,217.6 179.8,240.6 150.2,240.6 131.9,217.6 138.4,188.8\" fill=\"#e0f7fa\"/><polygon points=\"270.0,176.0 294.0,186.0 304.0,210.0 294.0,234.0 270.0,244.0 246.0,234.0 236.0,210.0 246.0,186.0\" fill=\"#fce4ec\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,316.0 186.9,324.0 198.5,344.1 194.4,367.0 176.6,381.9 153.4,381.9 135.6,367.0 131.5,344.1 143.1,324.0\" fill=\"#fff9c4\"/></g><g font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1b5e20\"><text x=\"60\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Triangle</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"12\">3 sides</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Quadrilateral</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"12\">4 sides</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pentagon</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"12\">5 sides</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hexagon</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\">6 sides</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heptagon</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\">7 sides</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Octagon</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\">8 sides</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"402\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Enneagon</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"418\" font-size=\"12\">9 sides</text></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: Polygons with 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Seven polygons, with 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 sides, labelled by the number of their sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: A table for the sides</b><br /><br />Pause and think: can we create a table to count the sides of figures like these? What would each row of the table show?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer: yes! Each figure can be counted in groups of its own number of sides. An octagon has 8 sides, so we can count by eights: 8, 16, 24... An enneagon has 9 sides, so we can count by nines: 9, 18, 27... We will build these two multiplication tables next.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_eight",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Eight (8)\n\nNow we turn to the table of eight. Multiplying by eight means adding the same number eight times.\n\nAn octagon has eight straight sides (<htcite0>). One octagon shows 8 sides. Two octagons show 8 + 8 = 16 sides. We are counting by eights: 8, 16, 24, 32, 40... Each step is one more multiplication: 8 × 1 = 8, 8 × 2 = 16, 8 × 3 = 24, and so on.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6d2e8f14-9b3a-4c7e-8d51-0a2f4b6c8e31",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Octagon",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 200\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An octagon with eight sides\"><polygon points=\"180.0,25.0 233.0,47.0 255.0,100.0 233.0,153.0 180.0,175.0 127.0,153.0 105.0,100.0 127.0,47.0\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" font-size=\"38\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2e7d32\">8</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: An octagon has 8 sides. Each side is one unit of our group of eight.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "An octagon with eight sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Sides of octagons</b><br />Each octagon has 8 sides. Suppose we want to count the sides of several octagons. Try different numbers of octagons:<br />• 1 octagon: 8 × 1 = 8 sides.<br />• 2 octagons: 8 × 2 = 16 sides.<br />• 4 octagons: 8 × 4 = 32 sides.<br />• 5 octagons: 8 × 5 = 40 sides.<br />Do you see the pattern? Each new octagon adds 8 more sides. That is what we call counting by eights: 8, 16, 24, 32, 40!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Eight\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>8</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Multiplying any number by eight is the same as adding it eight times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “eight times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The answer is always N added to itself eight times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by eight at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 8 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you do, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch every answer for the table of eight appear.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent8\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Three octagons</b><br /><br />Pause and think: if one octagon has 8 sides, how many sides do 3 octagons have?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer: three octagons have 8 + 8 + 8 = 24 sides, so 8 × 3 = 24. Counting by eights three times also gives 8, 16, 24.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_nine",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Nine (9)\n\nNow we turn to the table of nine. Multiplying by nine means adding the same number nine times.\n\nAn enneagon, also called a nonagon, has nine straight sides (<htcite0>). One enneagon shows 9 sides. Two enneagons show 9 + 9 = 18 sides. We are counting by nines: 9, 18, 27, 36, 45... Each step is one more multiplication: 9 × 1 = 9, 9 × 2 = 18, 9 × 3 = 27, and so on.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c4a9e7b3-5d1f-4a82-9c6e-7b3d0f2a5c94",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Nonagon",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 210\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An enneagon with nine sides\"><polygon points=\"180.0,27.0 230.1,45.2 256.8,91.5 247.5,144.0 206.7,178.3 153.3,178.3 112.5,144.0 103.2,91.5 129.9,45.2\" fill=\"#fff3e0\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" font-size=\"38\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2e7d32\">9</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: An enneagon has 9 sides. Each side is one unit of our group of nine.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "An enneagon with nine sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 2: Sides of enneagons</b><br />Each enneagon has 9 sides. Suppose we want to count the sides of several enneagons. Try different numbers of enneagons:<br />• 1 enneagon: 9 × 1 = 9 sides.<br />• 2 enneagons: 9 × 2 = 18 sides.<br />• 4 enneagons: 9 × 4 = 36 sides.<br />• 5 enneagons: 9 × 5 = 45 sides.<br />Do you see the pattern? Each new enneagon adds 9 more sides. That is what we call counting by nines: 9, 18, 27, 36, 45!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Nine\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>9</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Multiplying any number by nine is the same as adding it nine times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “nine times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The answer is always N added to itself nine times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by nine at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 9 — exactly what we saw in the table of zero and the table of one.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you do, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch every answer for the table of nine appear.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent9\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: How much is 9 × 8?</b><br /><br />Pause and think: count by nines eight times — 9, 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72 — or use the table of nine to find the answer.<br /><br />Then check: is the result the same as 8 × 9?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer: counting by nines eight times gives 9, 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, so 9 × 8 = 72. And 8 × 9 = 72 too (<htcite0>): counting by eights nine times gives 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 48, 56, 64, 72. The answer stays the same when we change the order.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nWe have learned two friendly new tables:\n\n- Multiplying by 8 means adding the number eight times, which is the same as counting by eights: 8, 16, 24, 32, 40...\n- Multiplying by 9 means adding the number nine times, which is the same as counting by nines: 9, 18, 27, 36, 45...\n\nWe used an octagon with eight sides for the table of eight, and an enneagon with nine sides for the table of nine. We also saw that 8 × 9 = 72 and 9 × 8 = 72. Changing the order of the factors does not change the product. (<htcite0>)\n\nKeep practicing with real objects — count the sides of octagons by eights, count the sides of enneagons by nines — and explore the interactive tables again. You will see these two tables work every time.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Does an octagon have eight sides?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "Yes. An octagon is a polygon with eight straight sides."
      },
      {
         "question": "Is 8 × 9 equal to 72?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "Eight groups of nine are 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 72, so 8 × 9 = 72."
      },
      {
         "question": "Does an enneagon have seven sides?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "No. An enneagon (also called a nonagon) has nine sides, not seven."
      }
   ],
